{
  "suite": "storage",
  "note": [
    "The paths are the ones a Supabase project answers on. The reference here is not a binary anybody can download: storage-api ships as an image, so the recording is taken from the one supabase start brings up, at the version in versions.json.",
    "Buckets, the byte path of the object surface, and listing. Move, copy, signed urls, resumable uploads and the S3 protocol are not asked yet.",
    "The fixture pins what it wrote, so a reading case is compared byte for byte. A case that makes a bucket cannot pin what the server is about to write, so those name their timestamps in volatile and the rest of the answer is still compared whole.",
    "Every case that changes a row says so, and the rows go back before it runs. The reading cases are written before the writing ones inside each feature for the same reason: a read after a write would be asked about whatever the write left.",
    "A case with no key at all is asking the gateway rather than the storage server, which matters because zou is both and the reference is neither: the image runs behind kong in a local project, and kong is what answers a request with no apikey.",
    "The object cases upload something and then read it, which the fixture cannot do for them: a fixture writes rows and an object is bytes in a store no sql statement can reach. So the reads after an upload are asked against what that upload left, and the writes that follow it say chained, which is the flag that keeps the rows from going back underneath them."
  ],
  "schemas": [
    "public"
  ],
  "cases": [
    {
      "name": "every bucket, to a key that can see them all",
      "feature": "bucket.list",
      "path": "/storage/v1/bucket",
      "key": "service"
    },
    {
      "name": "one bucket by its id",
      "feature": "bucket.get",
      "path": "/storage/v1/bucket/photos",
      "key": "service"
    },
    {
      "name": "the public one, so the flag is read rather than assumed",
      "feature": "bucket.get",
      "path": "/storage/v1/bucket/notes",
      "key": "service",
      "note": "two buckets differing in one field is the only way a field that is always false proves anything"
    },
    {
      "name": "a bucket that is not there",
      "feature": "bucket.get",
      "path": "/storage/v1/bucket/nowhere",
      "key": "service"
    },
    {
      "name": "the bucket list an anon key sees",
      "feature": "bucket.list",
      "path": "/storage/v1/bucket",
      "key": "anon",
      "note": "no policy on storage.buckets in a project that has changed nothing, so what comes back is what row level security leaves"
    },
    {
      "name": "a bucket an anon key asks for by id",
      "feature": "bucket.get",
      "path": "/storage/v1/bucket/photos",
      "key": "anon"
    },
    {
      "name": "the bucket list with no key at all",
      "feature": "bucket.list",
      "path": "/storage/v1/bucket",
      "key": "none"
    },
    {
      "name": "a bearer that is not a token",
      "feature": "bucket.list",
      "path": "/storage/v1/bucket",
      "key": "anon",
      "headers": {
        "authorization": "Bearer not-a-token"
      }
    },
